-- std.error — the standard-library error value (Tier 1, no stdlib imports).
--
-- `Error { code: Text, message: Text }` is the error half of every
-- capability-bearing standard-library `Result` — the file verbs, the net verbs,
-- the process and data layers all fail with one. The `code` is the machine
-- channel (`enoent`, `db.error.23505`) that callers match on; the `message` is
-- the human sentence describing what happened.
--
-- An Error is produced by the runtime, never built in Ridge: it has no
-- constructor, so a value of this type always comes back from a verb that
-- failed. Its fields are read the usual way (`e.code`, `e.message`).
--
-- This module exists so an error can be rendered, which is what most programs
-- do with one the moment they catch it.

-- Render an error as `code: message` — the classifier first, then the sentence.
--
-- The code leads because an interpolated error almost always ends up in a log
-- line, and the code is what identifies the failure there: it is the same
-- channel the standard library itself sorts on. Rust (`No such file or
-- directory (os error 2)`), Python (`[Errno 2] No such file …`) and Java
-- (`java.io.FileNotFoundException: x.txt`) all keep the classifier alongside
-- the human text for the same reason.
--
-- Either half may be absent, and an empty one is dropped along with its
-- separator rather than rendered as a dangling `code: ` or `: message`. No
-- producer leaves one empty today — every error the runtime builds goes through
-- one constructor that fills both — so those two branches are a guard against a
-- future one, not a case anyone can reach from here.
pub fn toText (e: Error) -> Text =
    if e.code == "" then e.message
    else if e.message == "" then e.code
    else $"${e.code}: ${e.message}"
